            "detailed": "RAM (Random Access Memory) has several defining characteristics, but 'Data is not auto-deleted' is NOT one of them - in fact, the opposite is true. Let's examine RAM's actual characteristics: RAM is VOLATILE - this means data IS automatically deleted when power is removed. Turn off your computer or lose power unexpectedly, and everything in RAM vanishes instantly. This volatility is RAM's most critical characteristic. RAM serves as WORKING MEMORY - it's where the CPU stores data and programs currently in use. When you open an application, it loads from your hard drive into RAM because RAM is much faster to access. RAM is TEMPORARY MEMORY - data resides in RAM only as long as it's actively needed. When you close a program, its data is removed from RAM to free space for other programs. RAM is also RANDOM ACCESS - any storage location can be accessed directly in the same amount of time, unlike sequential access storage. Think of RAM as your desk workspace: it's where you keep papers you're actively working on (temporary), you can quickly grab any paper you need (random access), but if the lights go out (power loss), everything disappears (volatile). Your hard drive is like a filing cabinet - slower to access but permanent storage. This is why saving your work is crucial - saving copies data from volatile RAM to non-volatile hard drive storage. Understanding RAM's volatility explains why unsaved work is lost in crashes."
