            "detailed": "The Machine Cycle (also called the Instruction Cycle or Fetch-Decode-Execute cycle) is the fundamental process that represents how computers perform computations. It's the basic operational cycle of a CPU and consists of several steps: Fetch (retrieve an instruction from memory), Decode (interpret what the instruction means), Execute (carry out the instruction), and sometimes Store (write results back to memory). Every computation a computer performs, no matter how complex, is broken down into these basic machine cycles. This cycle repeats billions of times per second in modern processors. Understanding the machine cycle is crucial in computer science because it reveals how high-level programming commands are ultimately translated into simple, repetitive hardware operations. It's the bridge between software and hardware."
