            "detailed": "Main memory (RAM) is directly accessible by the CPU, meaning the CPU can read from and write to RAM without any intermediate steps. This direct access is what makes RAM so fast and why it's used as the primary working memory. The CPU has dedicated buses (data pathways) that connect it directly to RAM, allowing for rapid data transfer. In contrast, secondary storage devices like hard drives and SSDs are not directly accessible by the CPU - data must first be loaded from these devices into RAM before the CPU can work with it. The CPU contains very small amounts of even faster memory (registers and cache) that are part of the processor itself, but when we talk about memory directly accessed by the CPU, we typically refer to the system's RAM. This direct access architecture is fundamental to the Von Neumann computer architecture used in most modern computers."
